{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:14.372Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399444 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062293 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399451 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1071 "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064711 1071 "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3km$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5km$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1000km/h$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186064711 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399470 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399424 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066490 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399464 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1071 "The equation $x^{2} - 4x + 4 = 9$ can be written in the form $(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $p + q$, and the sum of the roots $x_{1} + x_{2}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186069223 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=9$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186069223 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=9,$ and $p+q=7.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+3=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080747 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186083619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083619 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186083619 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399375 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399483 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399417 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095243 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399477 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1071 "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186097224 1071 "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186097224 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399396 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102776 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106155 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106155 1074 "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-y+4z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-1+y-4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186110658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399437 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399457 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399497 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399518 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399490 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399504 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399511 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399525 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113202 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186115711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399431 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115711 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = 1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186115711 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115711 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113592 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186115776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399551 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115776 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t ∈ (0, π)$?\n\n$f(t)|= ⟨\\sin (2t), \\cos t, 2 - \\cos (2t)⟩; g(t)|= ⟨1 + t^{2},-t + 2, \\sqrt{t}⟩; h(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{t}, \\tan t, \\cos t⟩$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186115776 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1+{t}^{2},\\,-t+2,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115776 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399544 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399563 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399602 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117307 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399557 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120196 1071 "Given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A = LU$, then $L =$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120196 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120196 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117403 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399570 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120212 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120212 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ N & N & N \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)$.\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120212 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,-5,$ and $4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117463 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399590 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120222 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨2t^{2}, e^{t}, 2e^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which $r^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120222 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120222 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117599 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399596 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120245 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨-5,0,1⟩+t⟨-3,1,0⟩,\\,Π:-3x+y-2z+27=0.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186120245 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n$l: r = ⟨ - 5, 0, 1⟩ + t⟨ - 3, 1, 0⟩, Π: - 3x + y - 2z + 27 = 0$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186120245 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y-2z+27 & =0 \\\\ -3x+y-2z & =-27 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ ⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ -3(-5-3t)+1\\cdot t-2\\cdot 1 & =-27 \\\\ 15+9t+t-2 & =-27 \\\\ 10t & =-40 \\\\ t & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨-5-3(-4),-4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(7,-4,1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399576 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399583 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126969 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399532 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$.\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-2$ and $λ_{2} = 2$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129586 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129586 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126975 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399538 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations:\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{2}(t)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129587 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129587 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399382 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399389 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399403 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399410 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399609 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220402 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186221966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399616 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186221966 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186221966 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1075 #ed